  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The findings reflect cities that already closely mirror 15-minute city conditions, which may limit direct transferability to car-dependent or spatially expansive urban environments.",
      "what": "Using a four-week time-use survey of 1,318 tracked participants across major Swiss cities, the study evaluates the spatial distribution and duration of individual walking routines. The authors developed a dual-center Exploration and Preferential Return (EPR) model incorporating home and work anchor points to simulate walking decay patterns. The results demonstrate that walking is heavily anchored around these two centers, with shorter durations observed among car owners, individuals without public transit passes, and residents of low-density rural areas.",
      "why": "It adapts human mobility modeling specifically to active transportation by introducing dual anchor locations, demonstrating that walking dynamics follow distinct spatial decay mechanisms compared to motorized modes."
    }
  },
